user_prompt:
Dataset collection: llm_causal_order_graphs
Dataset name: survey_paper_repo

Variables are listed below in random order. Use the exact id strings.
[
  {
    "id": "Education",
    "description": "up to high school or university degree",
    "states": "",
    "state_count": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "Residence",
    "description": "the size of the city the individual lives in, recorded as either small or big",
    "states": "",
    "state_count": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "Age",
    "description": "Age of people using transport",
    "states": "",
    "state_count": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "Travel",
    "description": "the means of transport favoured by the individual",
    "states": "",
    "state_count": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "Occupation",
    "description": "employee or self-employed",
    "states": "",
    "state_count": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "Sex",
    "description": "male or female",
    "states": "",
    "state_count": ""
  }
]

Task: propose a causal order prior. Earlier variables are potential causes or upstream conditions; later variables are downstream effects, measurements, symptoms, decisions, or aggregate outcomes.

Return one JSON object with this schema:
{
  "ordered_nodes": ["exact id 1", "exact id 2", "..."],
  "confidence": 0.0,
  "high_confidence_pairs": [
    {"before": "exact id", "after": "exact id", "confidence": 0.0}
  ],
  "rationale": "brief, no more than 80 words"
}

Constraints: ordered_nodes must contain every provided id exactly once; do not invent nodes; if evidence is weak, still provide the best semantic prior and lower the confidence.

raw_response:
{
  "ordered_nodes": ["Sex", "Age", "Education", "Occupation", "Residence", "Travel"],
  "confidence": 0.62,
  "high_confidence_pairs": [
    {"before": "Sex", "after": "Education", "confidence": 0.86},
    {"before": "Sex", "after": "Occupation", "confidence": 0.82},
    {"before": "Age", "after": "Occupation", "confidence": 0.9},
    {"before": "Education", "after": "Occupation", "confidence": 0.78},
    {"before": "Occupation", "after": "Travel", "confidence": 0.74},
    {"before": "Residence", "after": "Travel", "confidence": 0.7}
  ],
  "rationale": "Sex and age are upstream demographic attributes. Education typically precedes occupation, and occupation can influence where one lives and transport choices. Residence may affect travel mode, but can also be jointly shaped by education and occupation, so the order is only a plausible prior rather than a firm causal claim."
}
